---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **「<작품> netflix which country」에 답하는 자리.** (`/netflix-which-country`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2026-08-29) ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * Search Console 실측에서 「어느 나라 넷플릭스에 있나」를 물어본 질의가 아홉 개, 노출 39회.
 * `/where-to-watch` 가 같은 자료를 쓰지만 그 지면은 **자료 전체의 생김새**를 말한다.
 * 손님이 물은 것은 **「이 작품, 어느 나라」** 하나다 — 물음 모양이 다르면 지면도 달라야 한다.
 *
 * ── 🔴 이 지면이 절대 헷갈리면 안 되는 것 ─────────────────────
 * **차트에 올랐다 ≠ 그 나라에서 볼 수 있다.**
 *   ✅ 말할 수 있는 것   「그 주에 그 나라에서 «볼 수 있었다»」 — 바닥값이다
 *   ⛔ 말할 수 없는 것   「지금 볼 수 있다」 · 「다른 나라엔 없다」
 * 넷플릭스는 나라별 편성표를 안 낸다. 차트에 없는 나라는 「없었다」가 아니라
 * 「10위 안에 못 들었다」일 뿐이다. 섞으면 손님에게 «틀린 나침반»을 주는 것이다.
 *
 * ── ⭐ 이 지면이 새로 싣는 칸 ─────────────────────────────────
 * 🔴 우리 도구는 작품마다 「이름만으로 한국 작품이라 가릴 수 있나」를 이미 재 두었는데
 *   (974편 중 koreaOnly 525 · shared 423 · unknown 15 · koreaUnconfirmed 11)
 *   **그 판정이 지금까지 어느 지면에도 안 실렸다.** 여기서부터 싣는다.
 *   ⛔ 「못 가른다」도 결과다. 숨기면 손님은 974편이 다 한국 것인 줄 안다.
 *
 * ⛔ 화면에 한국어를 내지 않는다 — 이 주석은 frontmatter 라 나가지 않는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';
/* 🔴 [2026-09-05] 기사가 이 지면을 가리키는데 이 지면에서 그 기사로 돌아가는 문이 없었다.
   한쪽으로만 난 문은 손님을 여기서 멈추게 하고, 로봇도 그 기사를 여기서 못 찾는다. */
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/kcw-which-country.json';
/* 🔴 [2026-09-06] 「넷플릭스가 차트를 내는 나라 수」는 손으로 안 박는다 —
   우리가 파일을 직접 세어 낸 값을 그대로 쓴다(94). 손으로 박으면 파일이 바뀔 때 거짓이 된다 */
import 사전 from '../../data/wikitip-tudum-dictionary.json';

const TITLE = 'Which countries had this Korean title on Netflix? What the weekly charts prove';
const DESC = 'Netflix publishes no catalogue by country, so nobody can list where a title is '
  + 'available. It does publish a weekly top 10 for every country — and a title on that list was '
  + 'watchable there that week. That is a floor, and here it is for the titles people ask about.';

/* 🔴 넷플릭스가 «주간 차트를 내는» 나라 수. 우리가 파일을 세어 낸 값이다 — 손으로 안 박는다.
   ⚠ data.marketCount(93) 와 다르다: 그것은 «한국 작품이 차트에 든» 나라 수이고,
     이것은 파일이 다루는 나라 전체다. 두 수를 같은 것으로 쓰면 한 나라가 조용히 사라진다. */
const 차트나라수 = (사전.files.find((f: any) => f.key.includes('countries'))?.countries) ?? null;
if (차트나라수 === null) throw new Error('차트를 내는 나라 수를 못 읽었다 — 이 절은 그 수에서 시작한다.');

const 찾은것 = data.asked.filter((a: any) => a.found);
const 못찾은것 = data.asked.filter((a: any) => !a.found);
const 몫 = (a: number, b: number) => Math.round((a / b) * 100);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  spatialCoverage: `${data.marketCount} countries`,
  variableMeasured: ['Countries with a chart place', 'Weeks on chart', 'Best rank'],
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/netflix-which-country">
  <h1>Which countries had it on Netflix?</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    There is no honest full answer to this question, and it is worth knowing why before you read
    the lists below. <b>Netflix publishes no catalogue for any country.</b> Nobody outside the
    company can list the countries a title is available in, and any site that shows you one is
    guessing or scraping.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    What Netflix does publish is a weekly top 10 for each of {data.marketCount} countries. If a
    title is on a country&rsquo;s list for a week, it was watchable in that country that week
    &mdash; that much is certain. So these lists are a <b>floor, not a catalogue</b>: every country
    named here definitely had it, and a country not named here may well have had it too, just
    outside the top ten.
  </p>

  <p class="warn">
    <b>Read the absence carefully.</b> A country missing from a list below does not mean the title
    was unavailable there. In {data.weekCount} weeks across {data.marketCount} countries, only
    {' '}<b>ten slots</b> exist per country per week, and Korean titles compete with everything else
    for them. Most availability is invisible in this data, and we will not pretend otherwise.
  </p>

  {찾은것.map((a: any) => (
    <section class="ttl">
      <h2>
        {a.hasPage ? <a href={`/title/${a.slug}`}>{a.title}</a> : a.title}
      </h2>
      <p class="sub">
        {a.type === 'Films' ? 'Film' : 'Series'}. Charted in <b>{a.markets}
        {' '}{a.markets === 1 ? 'country' : 'countries'}</b> across {a.weeks}
        {' '}{a.weeks === 1 ? 'week' : 'weeks'}, {a.firstWeek} to {a.lastWeek}.
        {a.peak ? <> Best rank reached anywhere: <b>{a.peak}</b>.</> : null}
        {a.atOnce && a.atOnce > 1 ? <> Most countries holding it in the same week: {a.atOnce}.</> : null}
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Country</th><th class="num">Weeks in its top 10</th>
            <th class="num">Best rank there</th><th>First</th><th>Last</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {a.countries.map((c: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{c.name ?? c.iso2}</td>
              <td class="num">{c.places ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="num">{c.peak ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="fine">{c.first ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="fine">{c.last ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {a.koreanVerdict && a.koreanVerdict !== 'koreaOnly' && (
        <p class="fine flag">
          <b>We cannot fully confirm this one is the Korean work.</b> Wikidata lists
          {' '}{(a.wikidataCountries ?? []).join(', ')} for a film or series with exactly this
          English name, so the rows above may belong to a different work that shares the title.
          We publish the figures and this warning together rather than quietly dropping either.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>
  ))}

  {못찾은것.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Asked about, but not in our files</h2>
      <ul class="cant">
        {못찾은것.map((a: any) => <li><b>{a.name}</b> &mdash; {a.whyNot}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}

  <section>
    <h2>For scale: most Korean titles reach very few countries</h2>
    <p>
      Of the <b>{data.titleCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korean titles</b> that ever entered any
      country&rsquo;s weekly top 10 between {data.weekFrom} and {data.weekTo}, the middle one
      reached <b>{data.medianCountries} countries</b>. <b>{data.oneCountryOnly} of them
      &mdash; {몫(data.oneCountryOnly, data.titleCount)}% &mdash; charted in exactly one country
      and nowhere else.</b>
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      So a list of three or four countries above is not a small number by the standard of this
      data. It is close to typical. We say that because a short list looks like a disappointment
      if you have nothing to put it next to, and that impression would be ours to correct.
    </p>

    {/**
      * 🔴 [2026-09-06] 오늘 커뮤니티 수집에 「I am SOLO 가 넷플릭스로 **190여 개국**에 나간다」가
      *   올라왔다. 그 꼴의 발표가 자주 나오는데, **차트 기록으로는 확인할 길이 자체가 없다** —
      *   넷플릭스가 주간 차트를 내는 나라가 94곳뿐이기 때문이다.
      * ⭐ 이 지면에 그 칸이 없었다. 「몇 나라에서 볼 수 있나」와 「몇 나라 차트에 들었나」를
      *   손님이 같은 것으로 읽을 수 있는 자리다. 갈라 놓는다.
      * ⛔ 발표가 틀렸다고 말하지 않는다. 우리는 그것을 «확인할 자료가 없다»고 말할 뿐이다.
      */}
    <h2>&ldquo;Available in 190+ countries&rdquo; is a different claim</h2>
    <p>
      Announcements often give a country count in the high hundreds &mdash; a show arriving on
      Netflix &ldquo;in over 190 countries&rdquo;. Nothing on this page can confirm or contradict
      that, and it is worth being plain about why: <b>Netflix publishes weekly top tens for
      {' '}{차트나라수} countries.</b> There is no public chart for the rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the two numbers are not comparable and never will be. One is where a title can be watched,
      which the distributor announces. The other is where enough people watched it to reach a
      published top ten, which is the only part anyone outside the company can count. The widest
      reach any Korean title has ever managed in the second sense is
      {' '}<b>{data.maxCountries} of the {차트나라수}</b>.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      We are not saying an announcement is wrong. We are saying we have no way to check it, and
      that a reader who sees 190 in a headline and a small number here is looking at two different
      measurements rather than a contradiction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this page cannot answer</h2>
    <ul class="cant">
      {data.cannotAnswer.map((c: string) => <li>{c}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">{data.floorNotCatalogue}</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to walk from here</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/where-to-watch">The same floor, for every title we hold</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/by-country">Every country, and how Korean its chart is</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/hit-or-flop">Where a title sits among all 974</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/netflix-korea-this-week">The Korean titles on this week&rsquo;s charts</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/spread">How a title moves from one country to the next</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/hard-markets">The countries Korean titles reach last</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title we hold</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwMethodLimits
    method={`Netflix's own weekly top 10 files by country, ${data.weekFrom} to ${data.weekTo}, across ${data.marketCount} countries and ${data.weekCount} weeks. A country is listed for a title when that country's weekly top 10 contained the title in at least one week. "Weeks in its top 10" counts those weeks. The titles shown are not an editorial selection: they are the titles that people searching this question actually landed on this site for, taken from our own Google Search Console query export for the four weeks to 2026-08-26.`}
    limitation={`This is a floor on availability, never a catalogue. Netflix publishes no per-country catalogue, so a country absent from a list here may still have carried the title outside its top ten — which is the common case. Catalogues also change monthly, and these weeks run back years, so nothing here says where a title is today. Netflix states no country of production; which titles count as Korean is our inference from the title text checked against Wikidata, and where Wikidata shows the same English name on works from more than one country we say so on the title rather than hiding it.`}
  />

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/netflix-which-country" />

  <style>
    .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .warn { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: .8rem 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent); }
    .sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .95rem; margin: .2rem 0 1rem; }
    .fine { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; }
    .flag { border-left: 3px solid var(--muted); padding: .5rem .9rem; margin-top: .6rem; }
    section { margin: 2.4rem 0; }
    .ttl { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 1.4rem; }
    .ttl h2 { margin-bottom: .2rem; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .95rem; margin-bottom: .6rem; }
    th, td { padding: .42rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: left;
      vertical-align: baseline; }
    th { font-weight: 600; font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em;
      color: var(--muted); }
    .num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
    .cant { color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
    .cant li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
    @media (max-width: 640px) { table { font-size: .87rem; } }
  </style>
</WikiTip>
